WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.772
The broadest split is categorical versus numeric.

00:00:02.772 --> 00:00:06.412
Categorical variables represent labels or ordered levels.

00:00:06.412 --> 00:00:08.567
Nominal and ordinal belong here.

00:00:08.567 --> 00:00:13.377
Numeric variables represent quantities. Interval and ratio belong here.

00:00:13.377 --> 00:00:16.967
Do not decide by looking only at the characters in the column.

00:00:16.967 --> 00:00:20.192
A ZIP code contains digits but is categorical.

00:00:20.192 --> 00:00:24.887
A satisfaction score uses numbers but usually represents ordered categories.

00:00:24.887 --> 00:00:31.237
Connect this idea to a university analyzing student records, surveys, and learning-platform activity.

00:00:31.237 --> 00:00:36.182
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:36.182 --> 00:00:42.982
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:42.982 --> 00:00:48.047
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:48.047 --> 00:00:53.069
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
